1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:03,500
A Greek historian was told how the Great Pyramid

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:04,700
was built.

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:07,540
A hundred thousand men, he wrote, working in gangs

4
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:09,160
of three months each.

5
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:09,775
Was he right?

6
00:00:10,833 --> 00:00:13,693
Let's count the builders ourselves, using what they left

7
00:00:13,693 --> 00:00:14,873
behind.

8
00:00:14,873 --> 00:00:17,753
Their town, their tombs, and the logbook of one

9
00:00:17,753 --> 00:00:19,233
of their inspectors.

10
00:00:19,233 --> 00:00:21,673
From the Giza plateau to a desert harbour on

11
00:00:21,673 --> 00:00:22,375
the Red Sea.

12
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:24,833
The ancient numbers only grew.

13
00:00:24,833 --> 00:00:28,093
Herodotus gave a hundred thousand.

14
00:00:28,093 --> 00:00:31,193
Diodorus gave three hundred sixty thousand.

15
00:00:31,193 --> 00:00:33,873
But Herodotus wrote, they said.

16
00:00:33,873 --> 00:00:35,393
Diodorus wrote, as they say.

17
00:00:35,393 --> 00:00:36,873
Neither man counted anyone.

18
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:40,153
And neither man was there.

19
00:00:40,153 --> 00:00:45,453
Herodotus probably reached Egypt sometime after four hundred fifty

20
00:00:45,453 --> 00:00:45,873
four BC.

21
00:00:45,873 --> 00:00:48,713
The pyramid was already more than two thousand years

22
00:00:48,713 --> 00:00:48,893
old.

23
00:00:48,893 --> 00:00:50,893
He stood about as far from Khufu as we

24
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:52,355
stand from Julius Caesar.

25
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:56,613
Read literally, Herodotus says even more.

26
00:00:56,613 --> 00:00:58,813
A new gang of a hundred thousand every three

27
00:00:58,813 --> 00:01:00,833
months means four gangs a year.

28
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:03,695
That is four hundred thousand men, every single year.

29
00:01:04,767 --> 00:01:08,307
Read him closely, and the word slaves never appears.

30
00:01:08,307 --> 00:01:11,207
He says the king compelled Egyptians to work.

31
00:01:11,207 --> 00:01:13,567
Ten years went on the road, and twenty on

32
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:14,580
the pyramid itself.

33
00:01:15,633 --> 00:01:18,433
Yet the slave story keeps being repeated.

34
00:01:18,433 --> 00:01:22,253
In two thousand ten, a news agency report said

35
00:01:22,253 --> 00:01:24,793
Herodotus described the builders as slaves.

36
00:01:24,793 --> 00:01:27,213
As we just read, he did not.

37
00:01:27,213 --> 00:01:30,673
The same report says Egyptologists blame Hollywood for spreading

38
00:01:30,673 --> 00:01:31,185
the myth.

39
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:33,447
Now the size of the job.

40
00:01:33,447 --> 00:01:37,107
The pyramid holds an estimated two point three million

41
00:01:37,107 --> 00:01:37,407
blocks.

42
00:01:37,407 --> 00:01:40,807
Together they weigh about six million tonnes, and it

43
00:01:40,807 --> 00:01:43,851
stood about one hundred forty seven metres tall.

44
00:01:44,933 --> 00:01:46,993
Not all that stone was local.

45
00:01:46,993 --> 00:01:50,493
Most blocks were quarried right on the Giza plateau.

46
00:01:50,493 --> 00:01:53,633
The fine white casing came from Tura, across the

47
00:01:53,633 --> 00:01:54,293
river.

48
00:01:54,293 --> 00:01:56,973
And granite for the king's chamber came from Aswan,

49
00:01:56,973 --> 00:01:57,968
far to the south.

50
00:01:59,033 --> 00:02:02,393
Aswan lies about six hundred eighty kilometres south of

51
00:02:02,393 --> 00:02:04,413
Giza, in a straight line.

52
00:02:04,413 --> 00:02:06,353
Some of its granite blocks weighed up to eighty

53
00:02:06,353 --> 00:02:07,353
tonnes.

54
00:02:07,353 --> 00:02:09,313
Even so, they ended up at the heart of

55
00:02:09,313 --> 00:02:09,913
the pyramid.

56
00:02:10,967 --> 00:02:14,367
Spread over Herodotus's twenty years, that is about three

57
00:02:14,367 --> 00:02:16,967
hundred fifteen blocks every day.

58
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:20,187
In daylight, one block every two or three minutes.

59
00:02:20,187 --> 00:02:21,719
So how many hands does that take?

60
00:02:22,800 --> 00:02:25,960
That pace sounds impossible, and some people use it

61
00:02:25,960 --> 00:02:29,020
to argue the pyramid was not built by hand.

62
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,080
Lehner's answer is simple.

63
00:02:31,080 --> 00:02:33,760
The stones did not go in one after another.

64
00:02:33,760 --> 00:02:35,365
Many teams worked at once.

65
00:02:36,433 --> 00:02:37,873
Lehner tested it.

66
00:02:37,873 --> 00:02:40,453
In a NOVA experiment, twelve men pulled a one

67
00:02:40,453 --> 00:02:42,513
and a half ton block across a slick surface

68
00:02:42,513 --> 00:02:43,913
with ease.

69
00:02:43,913 --> 00:02:46,053
Moving stone did not need armies.

70
00:02:46,053 --> 00:02:47,633
It needed well drilled teams.

71
00:02:48,700 --> 00:02:50,800
From there he worked out the rest.

72
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:53,320
About twelve hundred men in the quarry, and two

73
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:54,820
thousand hauling.

74
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:57,140
Add the stone setters, and the total for twenty

75
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:59,623
years of work is five thousand men or fewer.

76
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:03,260
The modern answer starts in the sand.

77
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:07,180
In nineteen eighty eight, archaeologists began digging a lost

78
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:08,820
town south of the Sphinx.

79
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:11,960
In nineteen ninety, a tourist's horse stumbled on the

80
00:03:11,960 --> 00:03:13,180
builders' tombs.

81
00:03:13,180 --> 00:03:14,076
Then came a logbook.

82
00:03:15,133 --> 00:03:18,033
The town covered more than seven hectares.

83
00:03:18,033 --> 00:03:21,353
The crown built it, with bakeries, workshops and royal

84
00:03:21,353 --> 00:03:22,833
grain silos.

85
00:03:22,833 --> 00:03:24,573
It looks less like a slave camp than a

86
00:03:24,573 --> 00:03:25,267
supply base.

87
00:03:26,333 --> 00:03:29,093
At its centre stood a royal administrative building with

88
00:03:29,093 --> 00:03:31,833
ten massive grain silos.

89
00:03:31,833 --> 00:03:33,853
Grain came in, and bread and beer went out

90
00:03:33,853 --> 00:03:35,133
to the workforce.

91
00:03:35,133 --> 00:03:37,473
In Arabic, the site is called the Wall of

92
00:03:37,473 --> 00:03:37,960
the Crow.

93
00:03:39,033 --> 00:03:41,713
The tombs point the same way.

94
00:03:41,713 --> 00:03:45,833
Zahi Hawass, then Egypt's archaeology chief, said slaves would

95
00:03:45,833 --> 00:03:48,013
never have been buried so honorably.

96
00:03:48,013 --> 00:03:50,913
Some tombs even held jars that once contained beer

97
00:03:50,913 --> 00:03:53,391
and bread, provisions for the afterlife.

98
00:03:54,467 --> 00:03:57,247
The skeletons also show medical care.

99
00:03:57,247 --> 00:04:00,587
Twelve workers had injured hands supported with wood.

100
00:04:00,587 --> 00:04:02,787
One man's leg was crushed by a stone and

101
00:04:02,787 --> 00:04:03,187
cut off.

102
00:04:03,187 --> 00:04:05,187
He lived fourteen more years.

103
00:04:05,187 --> 00:04:08,387
The workers he studied died between thirty and thirty

104
00:04:08,387 --> 00:04:09,016
five.

105
00:04:10,067 --> 00:04:12,867
But not slaves is not the same as free.

106
00:04:12,867 --> 00:04:16,927
The archaeologist Mark Lehner asks whether they were conscripts.

107
00:04:16,927 --> 00:04:20,227
Hawass describes crews rotating in, three months at a

108
00:04:20,227 --> 00:04:20,520
time.

109
00:04:21,600 --> 00:04:24,280
The builders also left their own names.

110
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:27,280
Gangs painted them on blocks, even in hidden chambers

111
00:04:27,280 --> 00:04:29,680
above the king's burial chamber.

112
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:32,040
One gang was the Friends of Khufu.

113
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:34,741
Another called itself the Drunkards of Menkaure.

114
00:04:35,800 --> 00:04:39,440
Lehner reads the graffiti like an organisation chart.

115
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:41,120
Each gang had two crews.

116
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:43,620
Each crew had five phyles, from the Greek word

117
00:04:43,620 --> 00:04:44,680
for tribe.

118
00:04:44,680 --> 00:04:48,540
Each phyle had divisions, with names meaning endurance, perfection

119
00:04:48,540 --> 00:04:49,261
and strong.

120
00:04:50,333 --> 00:04:53,693
That logbook belonged to an inspector named Merer.

121
00:04:53,693 --> 00:04:55,993
He ran a crew of about forty men, late

122
00:04:55,993 --> 00:04:57,753
in King Khufu's reign.

123
00:04:57,753 --> 00:04:59,912
His logbook records the work day by day.

124
00:05:00,967 --> 00:05:04,447
Merer's journal was found in two thousand thirteen, at

125
00:05:04,447 --> 00:05:07,007
an ancient harbour on the Red Sea coast.

126
00:05:07,007 --> 00:05:10,867
That is about a hundred nineteen kilometres south of

127
00:05:10,867 --> 00:05:10,867
Suez.

128
00:05:10,867 --> 00:05:14,347
It is the oldest surviving papyrus document, more than

129
00:05:14,347 --> 00:05:15,601
four and a half thousand years old.

130
00:05:16,667 --> 00:05:19,427
They shipped fine limestone by boat from the quarries

131
00:05:19,427 --> 00:05:21,607
at Tura to the pyramid.

132
00:05:21,607 --> 00:05:24,007
One boat may have carried about thirty blocks.

133
00:05:24,007 --> 00:05:26,011
The short round trip took about two days.

134
00:05:27,067 --> 00:05:30,027
Merer's trips ran from July to November.

135
00:05:30,027 --> 00:05:32,707
That was the flood season, when the Nile rose

136
00:05:32,707 --> 00:05:35,147
by six or seven metres.

137
00:05:35,147 --> 00:05:37,567
High water was the only practical time for big

138
00:05:37,567 --> 00:05:38,267
cargo boats.

139
00:05:39,333 --> 00:05:42,093
Crews also combined for big jobs.

140
00:05:42,093 --> 00:05:45,153
For ten days, Merer's men worked on a dam.

141
00:05:45,153 --> 00:05:48,153
On one day they joined about fifteen other phyles,

142
00:05:48,153 --> 00:05:50,373
perhaps six hundred more men.

143
00:05:50,373 --> 00:05:53,813
Their supervisor was most likely Ankh-haf, overseer of all

144
00:05:53,813 --> 00:05:54,672
the king's works.

145
00:05:55,733 --> 00:05:59,113
Back at the town, long galleries look like barracks.

146
00:05:59,113 --> 00:06:01,513
A crew of about forty, like Merer's, would just

147
00:06:01,513 --> 00:06:02,873
fit inside one.

148
00:06:02,873 --> 00:06:05,035
The workforce came in units we can count.

149
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:09,820
Modern estimates disagree, but look at their size.

150
00:06:09,820 --> 00:06:12,900
Lehner counts about five thousand core builders.

151
00:06:12,900 --> 00:06:16,560
Hawass has said twenty thousand, and thirty six thousand.

152
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:19,668
A contractor's ten year schedule peaks at forty thousand.

153
00:06:20,733 --> 00:06:24,293
Hawass sees the pyramid as a national project.

154
00:06:24,293 --> 00:06:27,093
Households, he says, sent food to feed the workmen,

155
00:06:27,093 --> 00:06:28,913
and everyone took part.

156
00:06:28,913 --> 00:06:31,313
It is a generous reading of the evidence, but

157
00:06:31,313 --> 00:06:33,384
it fits the bakeries and the silos.

158
00:06:34,467 --> 00:06:38,107
Even the highest modern estimate is well under Herodotus's

159
00:06:38,107 --> 00:06:39,567
hundred thousand.

160
00:06:39,567 --> 00:06:42,007
His figure is two and a half times bigger,

161
00:06:42,007 --> 00:06:44,667
and Diodorus's is nine times.

162
00:06:44,667 --> 00:06:47,007
They measured different things, but they were the wrong

163
00:06:47,007 --> 00:06:47,523
size.

164
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:50,640
Here is what we still don't know.

165
00:06:50,640 --> 00:06:53,060
How many men cut stone far away, at Aswan

166
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:53,580
and Tura.

167
00:06:53,580 --> 00:06:55,600
How many blocks there really are.

168
00:06:55,600 --> 00:06:59,220
Even Hawass has given figures from ten thousand to

169
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:00,547
thirty six thousand.

170
00:07:01,600 --> 00:07:04,060
So, a hundred thousand men in gangs of three

171
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:05,040
months?

172
00:07:05,040 --> 00:07:06,020
No.

173
00:07:06,020 --> 00:07:08,560
Thousands, tens of thousands at the peak, and every

174
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:09,500
one of them had to eat.

175
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:13,540
Hawass estimated twenty one cattle and twenty three sheep

176
00:07:13,540 --> 00:07:13,952
a day.

177
00:07:15,033 --> 00:07:17,193
Over a year, that is more than seven thousand

178
00:07:17,193 --> 00:07:19,073
six hundred cattle.

179
00:07:19,073 --> 00:07:21,153
Moving the stone was hard.

180
00:07:21,153 --> 00:07:22,653
Feeding the people who moved it may have been

181
00:07:22,653 --> 00:07:23,460
the bigger job.

182
00:07:24,533 --> 00:07:26,973
The Great Pyramid was not built by a vast

183
00:07:26,973 --> 00:07:28,213
slave army.

184
00:07:28,213 --> 00:07:31,733
It was built by organised crews, housed, fed and

185
00:07:31,733 --> 00:07:33,133
buried with honour.

186
00:07:33,133 --> 00:07:36,093
Next, why half of Rome survived nearly a thousand

187
00:07:36,093 --> 00:07:36,907
years longer.
